  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $u(t)={t}^{2}+1$ is continuous at $t=2$ and \n\n$$\nu(2)={2}^{2}+1=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(u)$ is continuous at $u=5$ and $u(t)$ is continuous at $t=2,$ the function $f(u(t))$ is also continuous at $t=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(u(t))=f(u(2))=f(5).$",
